{:edb/db-before-t 1805 :edb/db-after-t 1806 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535118 50 #inst "2026-10-08T17:17:50.255Z" 13194139535118 true] [17592186332144 1100 "Find the constant particular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" 13194139535118 true] [17592186332144 1100 "Find the singular solution to the following Clairaut differential equation:\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3.\n$$" 13194139535118 false] [17592186332144 1145 "A Clairaut differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+g({y}^{′}),\n$$\n\nwhere $g$ is a differentiable function of ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTo solve the Clairaut differential equation\n\n$$\ny=x{y}^{′}+{y}^{′}-3,\n$$\n\nwe start by setting $p={y}^{′}.$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation gives\n\n$$\ny=xp+p-3.\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =(xp+p-3)^{′} \\\\ p & =p+x{p}^{′}+{p}^{′} \\\\ 0 & ={p}^{′}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe product on the right-hand side equals zero, so at least one factor must be zero.\n\nThe singular solution of the Clairaut differential equation is obtained if $x+1=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\nx=-1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute this into $y=xp+p-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =xp+p-3 \\\\  & =(-1)p+p-3 \\\\  & =-p+p-3 \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the explicit form of the singular solution is\n\n$$\ny=-3.\n$$" 13194139535118 false] [17592186332144 1145 "A constant particular solution has $y'=0$. Substituting this into\n\n$$y=xy'+y'-3$$\n\ngives $y=-3$. Its derivative is zero, and direct substitution verifies\n\n$$-3=x\\cdot0+0-3.$$\n\nTherefore, the constant particular solution is $y=-3$.\n\nTo distinguish this from a singular solution, write $p=y'$. The equation becomes $y=(x+1)p-3$, and differentiation gives $0=(x+1)p'$. The ordinary straight-line family is\n\n$$y=c(x+1)-3,$$\n\nwith arbitrary constant $c$. The envelope condition obtained by differentiating this family with respect to $c$ is $x+1=0$. It forces $x=-1$ and $y=-3$, giving a single point rather than a singular graph $y(x)$. The constant solution $y=-3$ is the ordinary family member $c=0$; it is not a separate singular solution." 13194139535118 true]] :edb/tempids {} :edb/committed false :edb/speculative true}
